#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bin_counts

#% text
# `bin_counts(xs, w)` counts the real numbers of `xs` in the bins `[k w, (k + 1) w)`, from the bin of the smallest to that of the largest. `bin_counts(xs, [a, b, dx])` counts in the bins `[a, a + dx)`, `[a + dx, a + 2dx)`, ... that lie within `[a, b]`, and `bin_counts(xs, [[e1, e2, ...]])` in the bins between consecutive edges (which may be `-inf` and `inf`). Elements that are not real numbers are left out.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The specification `[a, b, dx]` (dx > 0) means the bins `[a + k dx, a + (k + 1) dx)` that lie within `[a, b]`: there are floor((b - a)/dx) of them, so a step that does not divide `b - a` leaves the part of `[a, b]` above the last edge out, a step larger than `b - a` gives no bins, and `b` itself is outside the last bin. For machine numbers a quotient within a relative 10^-12 of an integer counts as that integer, and the last edge is then `b`. `bin_lists`, `histogram` and `histogram_list` read the specification the same way.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A step of 3 over [0, 10] gives the three bins [0, 3), [3, 6), [6, 9); 9.5 and 10 lie in none:

#% cell
bin_counts([1, 2, 5, 9, 9.5, 10], [0, 10, 3])
#% output
#= [2, 1, 0]

#% cell
bin_counts([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], 2)
#% output
#= [2, 3, 2, 1]

#% cell
bin_counts([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], [0, 10, 1])
#% output
#= [0, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0]

#% cell
bin_counts([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], [[-inf, 2, 5, 7, inf]])
#% output
#= [2, 4, 2, 0]

#% cell
bin_counts([1.5, 3, a, 2.5, 1, im], 2)
#% output
#= [2, 2]

